{"id":"H1","statement":"In logistic regression models trained on imbalanced binary classification datasets, random oversampling of the minority class will lead to a higher minority-class recall than class weighting, when evaluated using the same model architecture, training data, and evaluation metric (recall) on public tabular datasets.","independent_variable":"oversampling method (random oversampling vs. class weighting)","dependent_variable":"minority-class recall","expected_direction":"increase","gap_ids":["G1","G4"],"evidence_ids":["E3","E4","E5","E8"],"rationale":"This hypothesis directly addresses the core research question by comparing two standard methods—random oversampling and class weighting—within the logistic regression framework. While E3, E4, E5, and E8 support that oversampling improves recall in various models, none evaluate it against class weighting in logistic regression. This hypothesis fills the gap in G1 and G4 by proposing a specific, testable comparison in the relevant context."}

